          %title Contents
          %blockquote
            %p under construction
          .standout
            %p
              On the formalization of the process of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
              – by
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} abstract nonsense
              – from classical
              %span.newWikiWord
                ∞-model
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/%E2%88%9E-model"} ?
              data to the corresponding
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
              \.
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#background_and_motivation"} Background and motivation
              %li
                %a{:href => "#general_ambient_structure"} General ambient structure
              %li
                %a{:href => "#the_charged_particle"} The charged particle
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#target_space_and_background_field"} Target space and background field
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#parameter_space_and_kinetic_action"} Parameter space and kinetic action
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#the_quantization"} The quantization
          %h2#background_and_motivation Background and motivation
          %p
            The search is on for the abstract formalization of the process of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            – the process that reads in a “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"}> classical field theory
            ” – for instance presented in form of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            or in form of a
            %span.newWikiWord
              ∞-model
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/%E2%88%9E-model"} ?
            background field data – and spits out the corresponding
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
          .standout
            %p
              There is an open problem of mathematical (-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"}> physics
              ) model building:
            %ul
              %li
                what is the true formalism behind
                = succeed "?" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            %p
              One that is to quantization as, say,
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+geometry"} symplectic geometry
              is to
              = succeed "?" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+mechanics"} Hamiltonian mechanics
            %p
              The formalism of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
              clearly suggests that the fundamental description of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
              is some natural operation on higher functors.
          %p
            While for various aspects and facets of this question there are well-developed formalisms – such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+quantization"} deformation quantization
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-BRST+formalism"} BV-BRST formalism
            – a full answer is certainly still missing, not the least because the full formalization of the
            %em question
            itself has still to be established.
          %p
            Considerable progress on this formulation of the question has been achieved with the formalization and proof of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+hypothesis"} cobordism hypothesis
            in
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/On+the+Classification+of+Topological+Field+Theories"} On the Classification of Topological Field Theories
            by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"} Jacob Lurie
            %em result
            of any full quantization procedure should be in that it clarifies what exactly a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFT"} TQFT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            is: a morphism from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-category+of+cobordisms"} (∞,n)-category of cobordisms
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi Bord
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z : Bord_n \to C
          %p
            In
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/On+the+Classification+of+Topological+Field+Theories"} On the Classification of Topological Field Theories
            Jacob Lurie indicates some first towards finding a similar formalization of “classical field theory” (in terms of his
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,n)
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Topological+Quantum+Field+Theories+from+Compact+Lie+Groups"} Topological Quantum Field Theories from Compact Lie Groups
            \. But for the moment, that, too, remains a bit sketchy.
          %p
            For the purpose of the present entry this indication of a quantizaton proposal by Lurie et al. mainly serves as a reference for the idea itself that a formalization of something interesting is to be sought here, and of the kind of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} abstract nonsense
            answer one hopes to find. We will however discuss a somewhat different-looking approach. It may well be related to the Lurie-et al proposal in the end, but for the time being we shall not concentrate on that relation.
          %p Rather, the approach for a formalization of the quantization procedure that shall be discussed at this entry here draws from a few different sources:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                The observation that a classical background field that should serve as the input for a quantization of a
                = succeed "-model" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi σ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/differential+cohomology+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} differential cohomology in an (∞,1)-topos
            %li
              %p
                The idea that by applying a pull-push quantization prescription a differential cocycle on a target space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                gives rise to a differential cocycle on a
                %em parameter space
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Σ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
                = succeed "-description" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
              %p
                The pull-push operation here is akin to that in
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+%E2%88%9E-function+theory"} geometric ∞-function theory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
                is obtained from a
                %span.newWikiWord
                  ∞-model
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/%E2%88%9E-model"} ?
                target space object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                by homming
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+cobordism"} extended cobordism
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cospan"} cospan
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Σ
                        %mi in
                      %mo →
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo ←
                      %msub
                        %mi Σ
                        %mi out
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_in \to \Sigma \leftarrow \Sigma_{out}
                into the target object and then pull-pushing
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+function+object"} geometric function object
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/span"} span
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %msub
                          %mi Σ
                          %mi in
                        %mo ,
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mo ←
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi Σ
                        %mo ,
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mo →
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %msub
                          %mi Σ
                          %mi out
                        %mo ,
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\Sigma_{in},X] \leftarrow [\Sigma,X] \to [\Sigma_{out},X]
              %p
                The main result of David Ben-Zvi et. al.‘s work on this approach is that they point out that as soon as the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+function+object"} geometric function object
                one uses satisfies the two
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+%E2%88%9E-function+theory"} fundamental theorems of geometric infinity-function theory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+function+theory"} geometric function theory
                as induced from the underlying geometry of configuration space objects
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\Sigma,X]
                as well as the
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+space+object"} loop space object
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %p
                The resulting pull-push operation is an example or a generalization of what
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"} John Baez
                discusses under the term
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoidification"} groupoidification
            %li
              %p
                The observation that a differential coccycle on a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentzian+manifold"} Lorentzian manifold
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
                gives rise to a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+net"} local net
                of observables, as used in the formalizaton of QFT known as
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/schreiber/files/AQFTfromFQFT.pdf"} here
              %p So the procedure discussed here regards differential cocycles on target space as classicai field theories, regards their quantization as a way to obtain a differential cocycle on Lorentzian parameter space, and identifies this as a quantum field theory by associating a local net of observables to it.
              %p
                These local nets, in turn, are akin to
                = succeed "s," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+algebra"} factorization algebra
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+chiral+homology"} topological chiral homology
          %h2#general_ambient_structure General ambient structure
          %p
            The ambient context is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi Sh
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi CartSp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H} := Sh_{(\infty,1)}(CartSp)
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} Lie ∞-groupoid
            = succeed "-topos" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,2)
            = succeed "-categories," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bousfield+localization+of+model+categories"} left Bousfield localization
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+functors"} model structure on functors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi CartSp
                    %mi op
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi sSet
                    %mi Quillen
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [CartSp^{op}, sSet_{Quillen}]
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msup
                      %mi CartSp
                      %mi op
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi sSet
                      %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [CartSp^{op}, sSet^+]
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CartSp"} CartSp
                is the site of Cartesian spaces,
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi sSet
                        %mi Quillen
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sSet_{Quillen}
                the standard
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+sets"} model structure on simplicial sets
            %li
              %p
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi sSet
                        %mo +
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sSet^+
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+for+Cartesian+fibrations"} model structure for Cartesian fibrations
                over the points, hence the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+model+category"} simplicial model category
                for quaasi-categories.
          %p
            For the definition of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/path+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} path ∞-groupoid
            functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{\Pi}
            and the induced theory of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/differential+cohomology+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} differential cohomology in an (∞,1)-topos
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/differential+cohomology+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos+--+survey"} differential cohomology in an (∞,1)-topos – survey
          %p
            When working with fibrant objects in the model, we will frequently use the constructions and notation from
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+fibrant+objects"} category of fibrant objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}G
            a fibrant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
            object in the model we write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbf{B}G)^I
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+object"} path object
            we write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi E
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{E}G
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi E
                          %mi G
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mi G
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mi I
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi d
                                %mn 1
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\mathbf{E}G &amp;\to&amp; *
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  (\mathbf{B}G)^I &amp;\stackrel{d_1}{\to}&amp; \mathbf{B}G
                  }
          %p
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi E
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{E}G \to \mathbf{B}G
            for the remaining map, induced from
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mn 0
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi G
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi I
                    %mo →
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_0 : (\mathbf{B}G)^I \to \mathbf{B}G
          %h2#the_charged_particle The charged particle
          %p We describe the general theory for the simple example of the charged particle.
          %h3#target_space_and_background_field Target space and background field
          %p
            The background field for the charged particle that we want to consider is the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the
                %strong target space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                – a smooth
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            %li
              %p
                the
                %strong structure group
                or
                %strong gauge group
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                        %mo =
                        %mi U
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G = U(1)
            %li
              %p a choice of representation
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ρ
                      %mo :
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
                      %mi G
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi Vect
                        %mi ℂ
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ,
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\rho : \mathbf{B}G \to Vect_{\mathbb{C}}
                      \\,,
              %p
                taken to be the canonical representation on
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                        %mo =
                        %mi ℂ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V = \mathbb{C}
            %li
              %p
                the
                %strong background field
                given by a
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    a
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+U%281%29-bundle"} principal U(1)-bundle
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi P
                          %mo →
                          %mi X
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \to X
                    classified by a
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycle"} cocycle
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi g
                          %mo :
                          %mi X
                          %mo →
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mi U
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g : X \to \mathbf{B}U(1)
                    in
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi H
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
                    which in the model is given by an
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anafunctor"} anafunctor
                    = succeed ";" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi X
                            %mover
                              %mo ←
                              %mo ≃
                            %mi Y
                            %mo →
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mi U
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \stackrel{\simeq}{\leftarrow} Y \to \mathbf{B}U(1)
                %li
                  %p
                    a
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo ∇
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
                    on this bundle, which in the model is given by a diagram
                  .maruku-equation
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mrow
                            %mtable
                              %mtr
                                %mtd
                                  %mi Y
                                %mtd
                                  %mover
                                    %mo →
                                    %mi g
                                %mtd
                                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                    %mi B
                                  %mi U
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                  %mn 1
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mtr
                                %mtd
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                                %mtd
                                %mtd
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mtr
                                %mtd
                                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                    %mi Π
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                  %mi Y
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                %mtd
                                  %mover
                                    %mo →
                                    %mo ∇
                                %mtd
                                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                    %mi E
                                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                    %mi B
                                  %mi U
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                  %mn 1
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %mo ,
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                          \\array{
                          Y &amp;\stackrel{g}{\to}&amp; \mathbf{B}U(1)
                          \\\
                          \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                          \\\
                          \\mathbf{\Pi}(Y) &amp;\stackrel{\nabla}{\to}&amp;
                          \\mathbf{E}\mathbf{B}U(1)
                          }
                          \\,,
                  %p
                    and whose
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
                    is given by the composite
                  .maruku-equation
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi F
                          %mo :
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi Π
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi Y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mo ∇
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi E
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mi U
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo →
                          %msup
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mn 2
                          %mi U
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %mo .
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                          F : \mathbf{\Pi}(Y) \stackrel{\nabla}{\to}
                          \\mathbf{E}\mathbf{B}U(1) \to \mathbf{B}^2 U(1)
                          \\,.
          %h3#parameter_space_and_kinetic_action Parameter space and kinetic action
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo =
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma = \mathbb{R}
            be the
            = succeed "," do
              %strong parameter space
            = succeed "," do
              %strong worldline
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentzian+manifold"} Lorentzian manifold
            and write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{\Pi}(\Sigma)
            for the
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/smooth+Lorentzian+space#PathnCategory"} Lorentzian path category
          %p
            To define the kinetic action, we first form the
            = succeed "-associated" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi g
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mi U
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi ρ
                        %mtd
                          %mi Vect
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi Π
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mo ∇
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi E
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mi U
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi E
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mi U
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %munder
                            %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                            %mrow
                              %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                %mi B
                              %mi U
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mn 1
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mi Vect
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  X &amp;\stackrel{g}{\to}&amp; \mathbf{B}U(1) &amp;\stackrel{\rho}{\to}&amp; Vect
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  \\mathbf{\Pi}(X) &amp;\stackrel{\nabla}{\to}&amp; \mathbf{E} \mathbf{B}U(1)
                  \&amp;\stackrel{}{\to}&amp; \mathbf{E}\mathbf{B}U(1) \coprod_{\mathbf{B}U(1)} Vect
                  }
          %p
            and then pull this back to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multisymplectic+geometry"} extended configuration space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \times \Sigma
          %p
            The morphisms in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
            category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
